WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:06.800
- I was always a firm
believer of whatever gift

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.700
that you have culturally,
you should be able

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:09.973
to make a life out of it.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:17.210
Right now, I'm working on dog
collars, beaded dog collars.

00:00:17.632 --> 00:00:20.500
I haven't made money
since March. So I got to

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:24.190
figure out other ways to take
care of myself and my family.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:29.190
So what better way to do it
then to use your own culture?

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:33.920
- [Reporter] As the COVID-19
pandemic stretches into

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.940
a second year, New Orleans,
a city hit hard early on

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.816
in the pandemic and whose
economy is dependent on tourism

00:00:39.816 --> 00:00:42.460
is trying to keep case
numbers down while also

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:45.950
figuring out what tourism
looks like in 2021.

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:48.590
To better understand how the
city's economic landscape is

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:50.660
impacting those whose
livelihoods are connected

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.370
to the tourism industry,
I spoke to a few people

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.730
to find out how they're
surviving financially,

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:57.350
and what's at stake for their businesses

00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:58.900
and their communities.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.600
- How important is tourism
to the New Orleans economy?

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.540
- Tourism is our major economic driver.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.630
Mardi Gras is a big, big deal.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.270
There is a tremendous amount
of tax revenue generated

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:12.000
so we are certainly missing that.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.780
- [Reporter] The destination
marketing organization

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:17.150
in the city says the tourism
industry was down 70%

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:19.490
in 2020 compared to 2019.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:22.200
But Jay Banks, a city
council member says safety

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.540
is the priority over finances.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.400
- I clearly understand
the economics of this, but

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.590
I'm also clearly understanding
the health concerns of it.

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:32.540
And while I would like to be able to say

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:34.989
that we can do everything
we do and keep people safe,

00:01:34.989 --> 00:01:37.067
unfortunately we can't.

00:01:37.067 --> 00:01:40.067
(Upbeat jazz music)

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:45.510
- Before the pandemic,
how many nights a week

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:46.343
were you playing?

00:01:46.343 --> 00:01:49.479
- I was playing like two
or three clubs a week

00:01:49.479 --> 00:01:51.980
before the pandemic.

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:54.430
- [Reporter] Renard Henry who
was born and raised in Tremé,

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:56.610
a historically black
neighborhood with deep-rooted

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:59.830
musical traditions comes from
an important musical family

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:00.663
in new Orleans.

00:02:00.663 --> 00:02:02.940
- [Renard] Justin Jones is my grandfather

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.937
and I have an uncle
Benny, he's the leader of

00:02:04.937 --> 00:02:08.765
the Tremé Brass Band. And I
have my brother Corey Henry.

00:02:08.765 --> 00:02:11.765
(Upbeat jazz music)

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:16.270
- [Reporter] Henry says the
gigs he's able to get now

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:18.360
pay less than before the pandemic.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.800
Recently, he performed with
Tremé Brass Band outside DBA,

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.240
a popular Frenchman Street
club that he used to

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:25.790
play inside of.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:27.830
- We're playing in the
DBA, we would average

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:32.830
maybe like $200, $250
with some tips, you know.

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:36.660
But nowadays we're playing outside.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.953
We're roughing it up, scrambling
like a little $40, $50.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:42.480
- [Reporter] Henry has
been able to find work

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.600
through a Mardi Gras crew
that has hired artists

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:48.210
and musicians as couriers,
house float designers, and more.

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:50.640
It's just one example of how
the city has come together

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.390
to help those who have taken
the biggest financial hit.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:56.657
(exciting music)

00:02:56.657 --> 00:02:59.210
- Hey, guys! How you doing?

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.070
- [Reporter] For Alexis
Graber, better known as

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:03.583
Trixie Minx, The Burlesque
Queen of New Orleans,

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:06.780
her business, Trixie Minx
Productions was doing well

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:08.210
prior to the pandemic.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:10.380
- That first quarter of
2020 was actually like

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.920
our highest grossing quarter yet to date.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:17.241
And we went from that to literally,

00:03:17.241 --> 00:03:21.290
it wasn't exactly zero, but like, I cried.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:22.980
- [Reporter] And almost
a year into the pandemic,

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.000
she's struggling to keep
her business afloat,

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.490
while also keeping her
employees working safely.

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:30.220
- I've tried everything.
I've done everything from

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:33.620
in-person shows to virtual
shows to zoom performances.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:36.030
- [Reporter] In October, she
launched a weekly virtual show

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:38.340
at DBA on Frenchmen Street.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:41.880
- All those shows went so
smoothly, but the problem was is

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:45.300
that we didn't make money. And
if anything, we lost money.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:50.140
And that quarter, I say
this a lot, it felt like

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:51.950
a sucker punch and a knife twist

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:54.010
and a kick while you were down.

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:55.970
- [Reporter] After a reset,
she scaled back the shows

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:58.045
at DBA to once a month.

00:03:58.045 --> 00:04:00.100
- I was super excited
about this, honestly.

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:01.970
Thank you guys for making my weekend.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:04.830
The hardest choice I had
to make was realizing we

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:09.290
had to do less, to be
able to save the business.

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:12.170
And it seems counterintuitive,
but in doing shows,

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:14.610
doing them safely, and
doing them regularly

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:16.460
actually lost the company more money.

00:04:17.710 --> 00:04:20.910
- Well, this is a live music
street first and foremost.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:25.910
That's what keeps it going.
And unless we all open,

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:28.990
it doesn't really do much good.

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:30.620
- [Reporter] Another
business owner who found that

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:33.480
being open was actually
causing her to lose more money,

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:36.240
is Sophie Lee Lowry, the
owner of Three Muses,

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.570
a restaurant and jazz
club on Frenchman Street.

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:41.161
- A regular Friday night,
you can't even get a table.

00:04:41.161 --> 00:04:42.070
- Mm hm.

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:43.270
- Or sit at the bar.

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:44.930
- [Reporter] But the pandemic
forced the restaurant

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:47.070
to close down in March
until they were able to

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:48.780
get a PPP grant.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:52.890
- I had used my savings, I
borrowed money from family,

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:56.760
exhausted every avenue I had until finally

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:58.160
the money came in.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:00.490
- [Reporter] She tried to
reopen for takeout and delivery,

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:02.480
but without the street's
regular foot traffic,

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:04.260
she found it wasn't sustainable.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:06.090
- Can you talk a little bit about how much

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:09.280
you're still paying to
keep this place dormant?

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:13.500
- Back in March, April, May,
not only did we have the rent

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.630
and utilities, we still
had purveyors that we owed.

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:19.840
So our income stopped March 15th,

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:22.050
but our bills kept coming in.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:25.260
It was somewhere around $30,000 a month,

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:29.630
just operating costs on
a shuttered business.

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:32.240
Thankfully, that's gone
down as our bills were paid.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:35.484
So now I would say it's around $15,000.

00:05:35.484 --> 00:05:36.317
- A month?

00:05:36.317 --> 00:05:38.500
- A month, for a closed business. Yes.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:40.170
- With zero revenue coming
in to balance it out.

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:41.559
- With zero revenue.

00:05:41.559 --> 00:05:42.392
(laughing).

00:05:42.392 --> 00:05:43.225
Right.

00:05:43.225 --> 00:05:44.940
- [Reporter] Her application
for a second round of PPP

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:46.810
was denied, but she's reapplied

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:49.540
with additional documentation
and she's holding off

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:51.760
on reopening until she gets funding

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:54.620
and the COVID-19 outlook improves.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:57.620
(lively drum music)

00:05:58.586 --> 00:06:00.550
- [Singer] Now roll with it!

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:01.670
- [Reporter] For many in New Orleans,

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:03.830
the pandemic isn't just
a threat to their health

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:05.230
or their bank accounts.

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:07.800
It's also a threat to
their cultural traditions.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:11.630
- You know, culture doesn't
get preserved for free.

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:12.980
- [Reporter] Shaka Zulu is the Big Chief

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:15.490
of the Golden Feather Hunters,
a masking Indian tribe

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:18.260
in New Orleans. He has
a few businesses related

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:21.260
to his culture, like a
gallery, a stage performance,

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:23.900
and a feather supply
company. On the weekends,

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:27.340
he teaches an African stilt
dancing class in his community.

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:32.110
- My life is, you know,
not just a cultural keeper.

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:34.300
This is what I do for a living.

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:36.530
- [Reporter] The pandemic
ground has worked to a halt,

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:38.900
but for Zulu, the questions
are bigger than when

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:41.130
he will be able to resume those projects.

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:44.670
- I think this pandemic
exposed what's been going

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:47.290
on for since the beginning.

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:48.960
- [Reporter] He says the
pandemic has highlighted

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.960
long-term inequality. How
dollars brought into New Orleans

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:54.260
by tourists don't always
find their way back

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:56.050
to the culture bearers who attract many

00:06:56.050 --> 00:06:57.960
of those visitors to the city.

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:00.410
- How come it's always
about us, but when it

00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:02.743
comes to economics, it's without us?

00:07:04.195 --> 00:07:07.195
(lively drum music)

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:13.360
The pandemic may possibly
bring attention to that,

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:17.470
because for once, everybody
is shut down, you know.

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:20.920
Nobody is open. So now, what's going

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:22.323
to bring the tourism back?

00:07:26.098 --> 00:07:27.390
(music ends)

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:28.702
- That's it! Party over.

00:07:28.702 --> 00:07:29.535
- Yeah!

00:07:29.535 --> 00:07:30.626
(applause)

